## Why This Is Asked
This tests cross-region cache coherence, multi-tier invalidation, and stampede protection with Redis and Memcached.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-datacenter invalidation with Redis Pub/Sub
- Per-entity invalidation keys and TTL design
- Write-through vs write-behind
- Stampede prevention with per-key locking and coordinated invalidation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Publisher
redis.publish('invalidate:entity:123', JSON.stringify({entityId:123, type:'user'}));

// Subscriber (cache layer)
redis.subscribe('invalidate:entity:123');
redis.on('message', (_, msg) => {
  const data = JSON.parse(msg);
  cacheEvict(`entity:${data.entityId}`);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle bulk invalidations efficiently?
- How to ensure at-least-once delivery of invalidation messages?

## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates your understanding of API design patterns, code reuse principles, and performance optimization across different API paradigms—critical skills for building scalable microservice architectures.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidates should discuss: a shared business logic layer, separate API adapters for REST and GraphQL, GraphQL data loaders for query batching, REST response caching mechanisms, and database query optimization tailored to each API type's access patterns.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Shared service layer
class UserService {
  async getUser(id: string) {
    return db.user.findUnique({ where: { id } });
  }
}

// GraphQL resolver with data loader
const userResolver = {
  user: async (_, { id }, { loaders }) => {
    return loaders.user.load(id);
  }
};
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests design of robust, multi-tenant cache invalidation with Redis and Memcached, covering versioning, streams, stampede protection, and testability.

## Key Concepts
- Versioned per-tenant keys
- Redis Streams or Pub/Sub for invalidation
- Stampede protection via short TTLs and mutexes
- Cross-service invalidation consistency
- Observability and test plans

## Code Example
```javascript
function publishInvalidation(tenantId, itemId, ver){
  redis.xadd(`cache_invalidate:tenant:${tenantId}`, '*', 'item', itemId, 'ver', ver);
}
async function readItem(tenantId, itemId){
  const v = await redis.get(`tenant:${tenantId}:item:${itemId}:v`);
  const cached = await redis.get(`tenant:${tenantId}:item:${itemId}:data`);
  if (cached && v === latestVersionFromStore(itemId)){
    return JSON.parse(cached);
  } else {
    const fresh = await fetchFromStore(tenantId, itemId);
    await redis.set(`tenant:${tenantId}:item:${itemId}:data`, JSON.stringify(fresh));
    await redis.set(`tenant:${tenantId}:item:${itemId}:v`, latestVersionFromStore(itemId));
    return fresh;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would this adapt to Memcached-only environments?
- How would you measure correctness and latency impact?
- How would you handle cascading invalidations across services?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design cross-protocol consistency, idempotency, and contract-driven evolution at scale across REST, GraphQL, and gRPC.

## Key Concepts

- Unified, idempotent write path across REST/GraphQL/gRPC
- Centralized idempotency store (e.g., Redis) keyed by client+request_id
- Event-sourced write model with an outbox for eventual consistency
- Per-protocol adapters and a single source of truth OpenAPI/SDL/proto
- Observability: tracing, rate limiting, and safe schema evolution

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: idempotent write handler sketch
async function createReview(req, res) {
  const idKey = req.headers['Idempotency-Key'] || req.body.idempotencyKey;
  const key = `idemp:reviews:${idKey}`;
  const cached = await redis.get(key);
  if (cached) return res.status(200).json(JSON.parse(cached));

  // validate and emit event
  const event = { id: generateId(), type: 'ReviewCreated', payload: req.body };
  eventBus.publish(event);

  const created = { id: event.id, ...req.body };
  await redis.set(key, JSON.stringify(created), 'EX', 3600);
  res.status(201).json(created);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test idempotency across REST/GraphQL/gRPC?
- How do you handle conflicting content with the same idempotencyKey?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of saga orchestration, CQRS, and event-sourcing in a tangible, beginner-friendly scenario with compensations.

## Key Concepts
- Saga orchestration across Inventory, Payments, Shipping
- Compensating actions and failure handling
- Event contracts, idempotency, Outbox pattern
- EventStore and read-model updates

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal event-contracts and command routing
type EventEnvelope = { eventId: string; correlationId: string; type: string; payload: any };
type Command = { type: string; payload: any };
const mapEventToCommands = (e: EventEnvelope): Command[] => {
  switch (e.type) {
    case 'PlaceOrder': return [{ type: 'InventoryReserve', payload: { orderId: e.payload.orderId } }];
    case 'InventoryReserved': return [{ type: 'ChargePayment', payload: { orderId: e.payload.orderId } }];
    case 'PaymentCharged': return [{ type: 'ShipOrder', payload: { orderId: e.payload.orderId } }];
    default: return [];
  }
};
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of cache invalidation patterns in a real, scalable backend with Redis/Memcached, focusing on data freshness, stampede prevention, and bulk invalidation across many keys.

## Key Concepts
- Cache-aside pattern
- Per-key locking to avoid stampede
- TTL vs explicit invalidation
- Bulk invalidation via pub/sub or background worker
- Data consistency in read-heavy workloads

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simplified cache-aside with per-user lock
async function getSummary(userId) {
  let cached = await redis.get(`sum:${userId}`);
  if (cached) return JSON.parse(cached);
  const lock = await redis.setNX(`lock:sum:${userId}`, '1');
  if (!lock) return await redis.get(`sum:${userId}`); // retry after data populated
  const fresh = await loadFromDB(userId);
  await redis.set(`sum:${userId}`, JSON.stringify(fresh), 'EX', 60);
  await redis.del(`lock:sum:${userId}`);
  return fresh;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for Redis clusters with replica lag?
- How would you test for race conditions and ensure stampede prevention in CI?
- How would you extend to multi-tenant invalidation and coordinate across shards?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a policy-driven, multi-source token issuance flow with per-tenant constraints and real-time evaluation.

## Key Concepts

- Lightweight policy decision point (PDP) for token issuance
- Per-tenant audience/issuer metadata and key rotation
- Context-based access controls: IP allowlist, geolocation, time window, device fingerprint
- Caching decisions to reduce latency and load

## Code Example

```javascript
// issuance after policy approval (sketch)
function issueJWTForPolicyApproved(payload, tenant, kid, ttlSec) {
  const now = Math.floor(Date.now()/1000);
  const exp = now + ttlSec;
  const tokenPayload = {
    iss: `https://idp.example/${tenant}`,
    aud: `https://api.${tenant}.service`,
    sub: payload.sub,
    iat: now,
    exp,
    nonce: payload.nonce,
    tenant
  };
  return signJWT(tokenPayload, kid);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test policy changes without affecting production tokens?
- How would you model multi-tenant policy conflicts and roll back safely?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross-service saga design in an event-sourced, CQRS system with retroactive business rules. Assesses orchestrator logic, idempotent handlers, outbox reliability, and safe, tenant-aware migrations.

## Key Concepts

- Saga orchestration across Order, Billing, Inventory
- Versioned event contracts and retroactive rules
- Outbox patterns and idempotent command handling
- Tenant-scoped read-model migrations and replay safety

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch: map PromotionUpdated to commands
function mapPromotionUpdatedToCommands(evt) {
  const c1 = { type: 'BillingAdjust', payload: { orderId: evt.data.orderId, delta: evt.data.promoDelta }, correlationId: evt.correlationId };
  const c2 = { type: 'InventoryAdjust', payload: { productId: evt.data.productId, delta: evt.data.promoUnitsDelta }, correlationId: evt.correlationId };
  return [c1, c2];
}

// simple dedupe check
if (dedupeTable.has(evt.correlationId, evt.eventId)) {
  return;
}
dedupeTable.insert(evt.correlationId, evt.eventId);
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you rollout the migration with zero downtime?
- How would you test idempotent replay for large event histories?
